able  magazines pistol grips thumbhole stocks  folding stocks grenade launcher flash  suppressors and forward pistol grips but  there was a way around this guns with  fixed magazines could keep these  features could be a bullet so gun makers  modified guns so the magazines could be  changed with a single bullet which met  the state's definition of a tool this  allowed them to keep the band features  and still sell guns with magazines that  could be changed without much trouble  this became known as the bullet button  loophole this bullet button modification  was legal in California for around a  decade then in 2015 two shooters stormed  a holiday gathering in San Bernardino  California and killed 14 people they  were carrying ar-15 style rifles with  bullet buttons they had bought in  California and then illegally altered  after the shooting lawmakers tried to  pass new legislation this time they  focused on closing the bullet button  loophole this incident caught the  attention of a state lawmaker by the  name of Steve Glazer and so he does  something to ban these bullets and what  he does is changes the definition of a  fixed magazine to one that cannot be  removed without the distance  three of the firearm the law passed  which meant bullet button guns were  banned and yet again modifications were  made so that more AR style features  could be kept  remember the AR style rifle with the  juggernaut tactical hell fighter from  the beginning  the juggernaut tactical hell fighter is  a kit that alters an assault-style rifle  so that users can change the magazine by  cracking open the action just a hair  like this gun dealers say this legally  counts as disassembly of the firearm  action so gun makers started here and  decades later after multiple assault  weapon bans they ended here California  compliant ar-15 as they're called she's  just the same as a normal and here  fifteen the bullets going just as fast  Smith &amp; Wesson Ruger cult and juggernaut  did not respond to a request for comment  a spokesperson from the National  Shooting Sports Foundation a trade group  for gun manufacturers said of the state  laws quote they're basing the bans  strictly on cosmetic features that have  no bearing on the operation or the  function of the firearm a spokesperson  for the California state attorney  general declined to comment on the  modified weapons
 

